{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Come with us and we will do good to you.</b> Here the commentators ask several questions. First: since Jethro was not persuaded to go with them by the first assurance, what did Moses add when he said to him a second time, ״וְהָיָה הַטּוֹב אֲשֶׁר יֵיטִיב ה׳ עִמָּנוּ וְהֵטַבְנוּ לָךְ״ — 'and the good that YHWH does with us, we will do to you'? Furthermore: why did he call him Hobab the son of Reuel? Furthermore: he said ״נֹסְעִים אַנְחְנוּ״ and ״לְכָה אִתָּנוּ״ — 'we are journeying' and 'come with us' — he should have said 'we are walking (הוֹלְכִים),' or 'journey with us (תִּסַּע).' Furthermore: ״וַיֹּאמֶר לֹא אֵלֵךְ כִּי אִם אֶל אַרְצִי אֵלֵךְ״ — 'and he said, I will not go, but to my land I shall go' — why do I need two instances of אֵלֵךְ? And as for what he said, ״דִּבֶּר טוֹב עַל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — 'spoken good concerning Israel' — he should have said 'to Israel.'" "Now it appears that the language of הֲלִיכָה ('going/walking') applies only to the movement of the legs, which transport a person from place to place — but it can happen that even while a person is walking to another place, his mind is still bound to the place from which he departed. But the language of נְסִיעָה ('journeying') — that relocation encompasses both his body and his thought entirely. For this reason the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Abraham, ״לֶךְ לְךָ מֵאַרְצְךָ״ (Genesis 12:1), for at the beginning surely his soul was still bound to his father's house. And in the end it is stated (ibid. 12:9): ״הָלוֹךְ וְנָסוֹעַ הַנֶּגְבָּה״ — first he was 'walking,' and afterward 'journeying' southward, for Elohim had weaned him from all of his father's house.\n\nAnd this is what is meant by 'and Moses said to Hobab' — he concealed from him the name Jethro, for perhaps people would think that converts are difficult for Israel, like an unsightly blemish (Yevamot 47b), for all that is 'extra' (יֶתֶר) is considered as nothing, and on this account he is called Jethro (יִתְרוֹ); and if so, presumably they would not treat him with proper honor. Therefore he changed his name from Jethro to Hobab, to hint to him concerning what is stated (Deuteronomy 10:19), ״וַאֲהַבְתֶּם אֶת הַגֵּר״ — 'and you shall love the stranger' — that he should be beloved (חָבִיב) among them. Therefore he asked him to come.\n\nAnd he said, ״נֹסְעִים אַנְחְנוּ״ — 'we are journeying' — for on our side it is called a 'journey' from the place from which we departed, for we have there no inheritance, no vineyard, and no family. But on your side it is called a 'going (הֲלִיכָה)' from your land and from your birthplace; and even so I request: ״לְכָה אִתָּנוּ וְהֵטַבְנוּ לָךְ כִּי ה׳ דִּבֶּר טוֹב עַל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that is to say, He commanded us to do good to whoever attaches himself to us. And in this context it is fitting to say ״עַל יִשְׂרָאֵל״ ('concerning Israel') in the manner that is stated (Isaiah 14:1), ״וְנִלְוָה עֲלֵיהֶם הַגֵּר וְנִסְפְּחוּ עַל בֵּית יַעֲקֹב״ — 'and the stranger shall join himself upon them, and they shall be added upon the house of Jacob' — for the term ״עַל״ ('upon') serves to express that which is an addition upon the main body.\n\n״וַיֹּאמֶר אֵלָיו לֹא אֵלֵךְ״ — 'and he said to him, I will not go.' He was vexed that Moses used the term הֲלִיכָה to him, suspecting that his thoughts were still wandering after his land and the place of his birth — for in truth the Torah was so precious to him that he would have disticated even his thoughts entirely from his land and birthplace, and he had complete desire to attach himself to the Shechinah." "And concerning this he said: ״כִּי אִם אֶל אַרְצִי וְאֶל מוֹלַדְתִּי אֵלֵךְ״ — 'but to my land and to my birthplace I shall go.' This is what may be called הֲלִיכָה, 'going' — when I go to my land to convert the members of my family, for then surely my thought will constantly be wandering after this holy place to which my soul is bound.\n\n״וַיֹּאמֶר אַל נָא תַּעֲזֹב אֹתָנוּ״ — 'and he said, Please do not abandon us.' I have never suspected you that upon returning to your house you would forget this holy station; rather, I was anxious because you see this people, for it is disposed to evil, and all their encampments are in murmuring and discord and complaint — perhaps on account of this it will not be your will to return to us. And this is what he said: ״אַל נָא תַּעֲזֹב אֹתָנוּ״ — for with the word ״אֹתָנוּ״ ('us') he sealed the entire matter and meant to say: I fear lest the abandonment be from us — ״כִּי עַל כֵּן יָדַעְתָּ חֲנוֹתֵנוּ בַּמִּדְבָּר״ — 'for on account of this you know our encampments in the wilderness.' And ״עַל כֵּן״ ('on account of this') is as Rashi explained, meaning: on account of the fact that you know our encampments — that all the encampments were in rebellion against the Shechinah, that they turned away from after YHWH — therefore I am anxious lest you not return to us.\n\nAnd even when you go with us, your thought will be upon the land of your birth, saying: Would that I had wings like a dove, I would fly and dwell in peace there — not like these encampments which are, as stated, in rebellion. Therefore I ask that even us — do not abandon, for through this you will be vindicating the many, and the eyes of all Israel will be upon you to learn a fortiori from you: if you abandoned your birthland and your high position and came to attach yourself to the Shechinah — all the more so Israel. And this is what he said: ״וְהָיִיתָ לָּנוּ לְעֵינָיִם״ — 'and you shall be to us for eyes' — and the vindication of the many will be dependent on you.\n\n״וְהָיָה כִּי תֵלֵךְ עִמָּנוּ״ — 'and it shall be when you go with us.' For the essential need for this matter is when you are going with us while your thoughts are wandering after the land of your birth, upon seeing the disorder that prevails among us — and therefore we need you especially, in order to vindicate the many.\n\n״וְהָיָה הַטּוֹב אֲשֶׁר יֵיטִיב ה׳ עִמָּנוּ״ — on account of the uprightness of our deeds — ״וְהֵטַבְנוּ לָךְ״ — and from what is yours they shall give to you. For since you will be the cause for the correction of our affairs and the vindication of the many is dependent on you — if so, who will eat and who will feel, besides you?\n\nAnd then Jethro fell silent and yielded to him, for the vindication of the many is great." "And according to its plain sense: at first Moses promised him physical good, in saying ״וְהֵטַבְנוּ לָךְ״ — and he did not mention the Name — and he did not wish to accept, for he said, I will go to my land, and there I will have much good. And afterward Moses promised him spiritual good — that he would be among the members of the Sanhedrin, who were called 'the eyes of the assembly,' as it is written, ״וְהָיִיתָ לָּנוּ לְעֵינָיִם״ — and he said, ״וְהָיָה הַטּוֹב אֲשֶׁר יֵיטִיב ה׳ עִמָּנוּ״ — he mentioned the Name regarding this spiritual good, for YHWH would place His spirit upon him — and he yielded to him.\n\nAnd so it was according to the opinion of our Sages, of blessed memory (Sanhedrin 106a), that of the descendants of Jethro some merited and sat in the Chamber of Hewn Stone, etc. — and certainly this was from the promise of ״וְהָיִיתָ לָּנוּ לְעֵינָיִם״."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:49.434537138Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}